WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.880
I reckon that this dome, here on a windswept
field in Denmark,

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.210
is the world's most frustrating work of art.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.820
I know that's a bold claim, so let me explain:

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.309
this is Elia, designed by an artist called
Ingvar Cronhammar.

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:17.019
He's famous for dark, industrial art, art
that looks like machinery,

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:19.480
art that looks alienating and remote.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.880
Elia certainly qualifies as all that:

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.679
it's been here near the town of Herning, in
Denmark, since 2001.

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:29.519
32 metres high, if you include those chimneys.

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:32.980
The steps up to the viewing gallery are uncomfortably
large,

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.630
it feels like it wasn't designed for humans.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.430
Comparisons to an alien spaceship that's landed
in the middle of rural Denmark...

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.770
well, they're cliché, but standing here,
they feel about right.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.050
Something feels wrong about this sculpture.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.980
Which is all very impressive, but that's not
the frustrating part.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.740
In the middle, just between those four pillars,
there is a gas burner,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.750
and it shoots a massive flame eight metres
in the air, a huge blast of fire.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.660
It's one more ominous, unsettling thing about
this sculpture:

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.489
it might burst into flame at any moment.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:06.880
But it probably won't.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.189
Because it does that, on average,

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:13.079
for just 25 seconds about once every two or
three weeks.

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:16.200
At random, decided by a computer.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.789
It might be months between eruptions.

00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:19.750
It might just be a few days.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.090
And according to an article in the local news,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.310
it won't go off if the wind is too high, which
is probably is today,

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.850
or if there are any people too close.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.920
Elia's web site doesn't tell you when the
flames are going to be,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.219
it just archives the time and date of all
the ones that you've missed.

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:36.770
They keep that up to date just to taunt you,
I guess, I don't know.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:41.069
And sure, there are a couple of photos or
videos from people online who've been lucky.

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:43.069
You can go online and see that.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.709
But you can also go online and see the Mona
Lisa

00:01:44.709 --> 00:01:48.420
in much higher resolution than you ever could
in person, in incredible detail.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.469
That doesn't stop people crowding into the
Louvre in Paris to fight with selfie sticks.

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:55.140
There is something about being there.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.139
So if Elia calls to you, if you want to see
the fire in person,

00:01:59.139 --> 00:02:01.569
you're going to have to camp out and watch
it,

00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:04.250
24 hours a day, maybe for months.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.399
As for me, I've got about an hour left

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:09.300
until I have to leave to catch my flight.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.700
So here's hoping.

